{"name":"Warm Onyx","hex":"#4c4845","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"warm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a somewhat high temperature, often but not always connoting that the high temperature is pleasant rather than uncomfortable.","Friendly and with affection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make or keep warm.","To become warm, to heat up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/warm"},{"term":"onyx","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A banded variety of chalcedony, a cryptocrystalline form of quartz.","A jet-black color, named after the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Jet-black; often, glossily so."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/onyx"}],"entries":[{"title":"Onyx","blocks":[{"text":"Onyx is a typically black-and-white banded variety of agate, a silicate mineral. The bands can also be monochromatic with alternating light and dark bands. Sardonyx is a variety with red to brown bands alternated with black or white bands. The name \"onyx\" is also frequently used for level-banded (parallel-banded) agates, but in proper usage it refers to color pattern not band structure. Onyx, as a descriptive term, has also been incorrectly applied to parallel-banded varieties of alabaster, marble, calcite, obsidian, and opal, and misleadingly to materials with contorted banding, such as \"cave onyx\" and \"Mexican onyx\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Agate-Quartz-49959.jpg/330px-Agate-Quartz-49959.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onyx","wikidata":"Q180884"},{"title":"Shades of black","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of black, or off-black colors, are colors that differ only slightly from pure black. These colors have a low lightness. From a photometric point of view, a color which differs slightly from black always has low relative luminance. Colors often considered \"shades of black\" include onyx, black olive, charcoal, and jet."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Color_icon_black_new2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_black","wikidata":"Q7460340"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg/500px-Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg/500px-Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cabochons of red onyx, also called sardonyx","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg/250px-Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg/250px-Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black onyx with white streaks","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Onyx.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/500px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/1280px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistor